  "summary": "Glossy, plausible tomorrows — the concept-art language behind Blade Runner's spinners.",
  "description": "Syd Mead — \"visual futurist\", his own coinage — defined the slick end of retrofuture: airbrushed renderings of plausible machinery, from US Steel and Philips product futures to Blade Runner's spinner cars, Tron light-cycle roots, Aliens' Sulaco, and Japan's Sony/Sharp concept commissions. Mead's grammar: strong single-light staging, believable wear on optimistic surfaces, people dwarfed by elegant hardware, and a rendering polish that made the impossible look like next year's brochure. Concept-art education still teaches from his books (Oblagon, Sentury), and \"Mead-style\" remains shorthand for corporate-future glamour with grit.",
  "culturalContext": "Syd Mead defined corporate futurism's visual ethics: his airbrushed plausibility (Blade Runner spinners, US Steel campaigns) made concept art a profession, his books teach rendering to this day, and \"Mead-style\" remains shorthand for glamour-with-grit in automotive, film and game design.",
